WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:08.459
Where did Seann William Scott go? He was everywhere
for a few years following the success of 1999's

00:00:08.459 --> 00:00:13.040
American Pie and its sequels. But around 2010,
it seemed like he pretty much disappeared.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.900
Here's what probably led to Scott falling
off Hollywood's radar.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.829
Unpopular with critics

00:00:19.829 --> 00:00:24.300
The bulk of Scott's films have been critical
duds. Though American Pie struck a chord with

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.980
critics and audiences alike, most of his movies
haven't impressed: Dude, Where's My Car? has

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.600
an 18 percent rating on Rotten Tomatoes, Dukes
Of Hazzard and Mr. Woodcock each have a score

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:40.350
of 13, Just Before I Go has a 10 and Movie
43 has a 4.. Two flicks—Goon and Role Models—

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.179
are the exceptions, with scores of 80 and
78 percent, respectively. You know what they

00:00:44.179 --> 00:00:46.319
say: two out of seven ain’t not bad...

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:48.489
Unpopular with audiences

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:55.449
Southland Tales had a $17 million budget but
only made about $273,000. Mr. Woodcock barely

00:00:55.449 --> 00:01:01.091
broke even. And Bulletproof Monk earned half
of its $52 million budget back. What do they have

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:03.420
in common? Scott in a leading role.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.330
He's Often Heard, But Not Seen

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:09.110
Scott has sustained his career with voiceover
work. The actor's pipes can be heard voicing

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:13.910
Crash in four movies from the Ice Age franchise.
Scott also voices Crash in the franchise's

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:18.390
TV movies, shorts, and video games. At least
if his fame isn't secure, his finances should

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.080
be. Considering they'll probably never stop making Ice Age movies.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:23.940
Typecast

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:29.520
At his peak, Scott was the goofy high schooler,
sleazy party boy, or college jock, even if

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:33.460
he was in his late 20s and 30s when he took
those roles. Now that he's older, he's not

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.530
up for those parts. Scott got the memo, though.
In 2011, he told MTV News that his role as

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.110
American Pie's Stifler may have, er, stifled
his career:

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.220
"I had so much fun and loved the character,
[but] I don't want to be known as that character

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.540
forever. Now I'm realizing that I probably
will be known as that character forever."

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.300
Personal Issues

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:57.550
In March 2011, Scott entered rehab. According
to TMZ, the actor voluntarily entered treatment

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:01.730
for unspecified health and personal issues,
staying for 30 days before filming American

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:06.260
Pie 4: American Reunion. It's unclear what
he was struggling with, as he hasn't addressed

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.759
anything publicly. Scott insisted to People
magazine in 2003:

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:14.620
"I don't really party. If I did, it would
probably just be trying a new bottle of wine."

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.430
Stiffler would be ashamed.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:18.140
Avoids Publicity

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:22.760
Scott often flies under the radar and is rarely
seen at events or photographed on the street.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:27.269
In an era of celebrity blogging and a 24-hour
news cycle, this otherwise respectable behavior

00:02:27.269 --> 00:02:31.829
may have actually hurt him. Scott's invisibility
between projects may have made audiences forget

00:02:31.829 --> 00:02:33.019
him.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:34.139
What's Next?

00:02:34.139 --> 00:02:37.060
Things haven't worked out for Scott the way
we thought they would since he first found

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.989
fame as Stifler. But that doesn't mean we've
seen the last of him. A Goon sequel called

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:46.280
Last of the Enforcers will hit in 2016, after
the release Ice Age: Collision Course. Maybe

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.620
Scott can call up his old pal Ashton Kutcher
and see about putting together a follow-up

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.870
to Dude, Where's My Car? Anyone up for Dude,
Where's My Uber?

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.959
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:00.370
just saw. And leave us a comment to tell us
why you think Hollywood's not calling Seann..

